WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
In the vastness of the Pacific, there's a place unlike any other.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Enchanted volcanic islands that are home to a remarkable collection of animals and plants.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Here, evolution is proceeding at extraordinary speed.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Galapagos.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:42.000
A place of wonders.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Millions of years ago, the islands were colonized by a strange cast of characters.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:13.000
But to settle in this harsh, unforgiving landscape, those new arrivals had to dramatically adapt their bodies.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Today, revelatory discoveries are still being made about them.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:42.000
And from their story, we can piece together how Galapagos came to be one of the most diverse environments on our planet.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
It's perhaps surprising that the Galapagos should have become famous for its biodiversity.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.000
For the fact is that living conditions here are very tough.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:01.000
On the equator, the heat is intense. There's very little water. Much of the land is covered by bare volcanic rock.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:11.000
And yet, every species that lives here is descended from an ancestor from the continents that have taken on these conditions and won.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:35.000
And the way in which they did so is extraordinary.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:45.000
The total land area of the 16 islands and rocky outcrops that make up Galapagos is less than half the size of New Jersey.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Yet for its size, there are more unique species here than anywhere else on Earth.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Why should that be?

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:16.000
There are clues hidden within the landscape.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:26.000
This crescent-shaped strip of cliffs rising steeply from the Pacific Ocean is the island of Tortuga.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.000
And like all the Galapagos islands, it's a wonderful place to see wildlife.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:39.000
Here and there, there's a sea lion. And above that, nesting seabirds, rubies and Galapagos gulls.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:51.000
But you only really appreciate the true character of this island from the air.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:04:01.000
From here, it's clear that this is the last fragment of an extinct volcano.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:11.000
These curving cliffs are all that remains of what was once a completely circular crater.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:19.000
And that is an indication of something very significant about all these islands.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:25.000
They change with enormous rapidity.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.000
The history of these islands is very much the same.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:46.000
Each is born on the bottom of the sea and rises up through the waters to emerge as a volcano.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:57.000
This is a typical Galapagos island in its infancy.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:06.000
But then, after a million years of eruptions, volcanic activity ceases.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Two million years after its first appearance, the island is approaching middle age.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:19.000
It's a moist climate and is covered by forest.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:25.000
It begins to sink under its own weight of ash and lava.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:32.000
It's battered by erosion and after four million years, it's near the end of its existence.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:39.000
Low-lying and arid with little rainfall, it's surrounded by beaches of soft sand.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:53.000
The waves and rain continue to take their toll until all that is left is a craggy outcrop of rock.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:59.000
These islands, in geological terms, are very short-lived.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:09.000
Today, there are islands in the Galapagos archipelago that illustrate every stage in this history.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:26.000
The youngest in the west are arid, black and still-breathing far.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:33.000
The oldest in the east have long since ceased to erupt.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:40.000
But each island provides its colonists with a range of habitats.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:53.000
And it is the youngest active islands that pose the greatest problems for any animals that attempt to colonise them.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:59.000
In the far west of the archipelago lies Fernandina.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:03.000
This is the youngest of the Galapagos islands.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:08.000
It's forbiddingly desolate and inhospitable.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:16.000
But one animal has colonised its shoreline.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:28.000
This creature is a specialist at surviving in this harsh terrain.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:39.000
And in adapting to this place, it has become like no other animal on Earth.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:53.000
Behold the marine iguana.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:26.000
The ancestors of these iguanas almost certainly lived in the jungles of Central America.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:36.000
There, still today, you can see iguanas in the trees overhanging the rivers, nibbling leaves, or on rafts of reeds.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:44.000
Just occasionally, some are swept out to sea, and the vast majority, of course, die there.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:56.000
But just a few, a long time ago, were fortunate enough to be swept by favourable currents out to the ocean and pitched up here.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:06.000
In their ancestral rainforest habitat, iguanas are vegetarians.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:13.000
Here, they browse on juicy leaves.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:21.000
But the iguanas that first appeared in the Galapagos could find no such things.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:30.000
So these iguanas, to survive, had to eat the only kind of leaf that was available.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.000
Seaweed.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:43.000
And to get the best of that, they had to do something even more radical.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:10:00.000
They had to swim.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:12.000
They even learned to dive.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:25.000
They acquired the ability to hold their breath for up to an hour, so that they could swim down to a depth of 20 metres.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:33.000
Their claws strengthened so they could cling to the rocks on the seabed.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:55.000
And under the water, they found an endless supply of seaweed, which grew in abundance in the nutrient-rich currents that flow around the islands.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:02.000
But that was not all.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:10.000
Their snouts became flatter to help them graze.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:39.000
Their teeth became sharper to grip the slippery seaweed.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:49.000
But cold water can be dangerous for cold-blooded reptiles.

00:11:49.000 --> 00:12:13.000
After a few minutes feeding at sea, the iguanas are seriously chilled and in urgent need of a warm-up. And their skin enables them to get that.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:18.000
It is black.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:29.000
Dark objects absorb heat, and each scale in the marine iguana's skin is like an element in a miniature solar panel.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:43.000
Now all a marine iguana needs to do to bring its body back up to temperature is to bask in the hot equatorial sun for an hour or two.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:51.000
But eating nothing but seaweed creates another problem. Too much salt.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:13:02.000
The marine iguanas dealt with that in a very particular way.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:12.000
They evolved a special gland in their nose.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:25.000
They simply sneeze the excess salt from their blood.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:34.000
These changes had to happen very quickly in evolutionary terms if the iguanas were to survive.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:42.000
But here conditions sometimes change, and then even iguanas struggle to keep up.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:52.000
Every three to seven years the weather becomes very extreme and irregular.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:14:01.000
It's a phenomenon called El Niño.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:09.000
And it can have a devastating effect on wildlife.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:17.000
Evolutionary biologist Marilyn Vitusek has studied the effects of El Niño on the Galapagos marine iguanas.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:21.000
She discovered that it decimates their food.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:23.000
Rest is 1.0.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:33.000
The iguanas usually eat red and green algae, and that algae dies off completely during El Niño, during strong El Niños, and is replaced by a brown algae.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:41.000
And marine iguanas aren't able to digest the brown algae. So they can eat it, but it sits in their stomach, basically in a big lump.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:50.000
And so you can find iguanas dead on the beach of starvation with their stomachs full of this brown algae that they're just not able to digest.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:59.000
The marine iguana is the worst affected of all Galapagos animals during an El Niño.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:06.000
As many as 90% of them can perish.

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:21.000
It's bad news for iguanas, but good news for scavengers.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:31.000
And new research has shown that iguanas have evolved a special ability that enables them to survive the famine.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:36.000
Their bodies shrink.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:43.000
They lose not just fat and muscle, but bone.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:49.000
The iguanas can actively reduce their skeletons over just a few months.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:56.000
So we saw that the largest animals were decreasing their body length by as much as 20%.

00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:05.000
The magnitude of that means that it can't be simply that they're changing their cartilage or connective tissue or resorbing muscles.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:09.000
Those things together account for about 10% of length.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:18.000
So instead, 20% of shrinkage really indicates that it's got to be the skeleton itself that's decreasing in length.

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:26.000
This amazing ability to reabsorb bone in times of hardship is unique to these reptiles.

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:39.000
It's the most recent discovery in understanding how the marine iguanas managed to survive on the coastlines of the youngest Galapagos Islands.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:49.000
But iguanas were not alone in adapting to these desolate volcanic shores.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:53.000
This is the larvae heron.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:56.000
It's well camouflaged.

00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:03.000
Its grey feathers make it relatively inconspicuous against the blackened larvae rocks.

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:10.000
So moving stealthily, it could hunt very effectively.

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:24.000
Its favorite prey is the sally lightfoot crab whose striking red shell stands out against the jet black larva.

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:38.000
But the adult crabs have tough shells and sharp pincers and herons know better than to attack a full grown one.

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:45.000
Juvenile crabs would be a much more attractive prospect.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:50.000
But they have responded to the landscape of Fernandina.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:18:01.000
They have black shells that make them far less conspicuous to prowling herons.

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:20.000
This time the larvae heron will have to make do with a really tiny snack, a sally lightfoot hatchling.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:26.000
On the shorelines of this infant Galapagos Island, life is tough.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:32.000
Habitats are limited, opportunities are scarce.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:40.000
But away from the coast, survival is almost impossible.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:50.000
There it's too dry and too hot for most forms of life.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:55.000
But in time, that will change.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:19:02.000
As the island ages, this hostile landscape will become a little more welcoming.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:11.000
It will one day support a rich forest full of new places for animals to live.

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:25.000
This change is driven by the volcanic hotspot which brought the island into existence.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:30.000
The eruptions continue.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:44.000
25% of its final bulk will accumulate in the next few hundred thousand years.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:55.000
By the time the eruptions have ceased, it's grown so large that it has acquired a new power.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:20:02.000
It has the ability to create its own weather.

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:10.000
Humid oceanic winds blowing across the Pacific hit this mountain of larvae and are so forced upwards.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:18.000
That cools them so that they can no longer hold their load of moisture and it condenses as mist and rain.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:24.000
And that allows plants to thrive.

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:44.000
The rose in the center of the archipelago is typical of these middle-aged islands.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:50.000
Its slopes are covered by a mantle of green.

00:20:50.000 --> 00:21:01.000
This might seem to be a forest of giant trees supporting a rich population of animals of all kinds.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:08.000
But this being Galapagos, this forest is different.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:13.000
These plants are not true trees.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:20.000
Trees tend to have big seeds and a few of those made it across the oceans to the Galapagos.

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:24.000
And certainly none up here to the highlands.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:30.000
But smaller plants have smaller seeds, some so small they can float on the wind.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:35.000
And one member of the dandelion family made it up here.

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:40.000
And without competition from other trees, they grew big.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:22:05.000
This you could say is a forest of giant dandelions.

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:12.000
This very special kind of dandelion is called Scalasia.

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:22.000
It's unique to the Galapagos and flourishes on the high slopes of Santa Cruz and other middle-aged islands.

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:30.000
It's become the host for a whole community that could not exist without it.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:41.000
Because Scalasia performs a conjuring trick that gives life to the rest of the forest.

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:45.000
There's no groundwater in these thin volcanic soils,

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:52.000
but the Scalasia trees are tall enough to collect moisture from the skies, from clouds and from mist.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:23:03.000
And that is sufficient to sustain a whole community of plants and animals.

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:13.000
High in the canopy, mist condenses on the spindly crisscross branches of the Scalasia.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:22.000
Water trickles down their woody trunks.

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:33.000
Ferns root themselves in the damp moss that clings to their bark.

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:46.000
The moisture creates conditions where spiders and other small creatures can live.

00:23:46.000 --> 00:24:00.000
And on the forest floor, pools appear.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:15.000
Here, dragonflies thrive and, once again, they belong to a species that occurs nowhere else but here.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:20.000
But the Galapagos climate is changeable, and the mists sometimes dry up,

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:28.000
leaving this delicate ecosystem exposed to the burning equatorial heat.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:35.000
Some trees, however, have evolved a way of protecting themselves.

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:42.000
This tree has developed a mutually beneficial relationship with the lichen that grows on it.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:48.000
The lichen shields the tree from the sun, preventing it from getting scorched.

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:53.000
And the tree provides the lichen with moisture and nutriment.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:25:02.000
But if the weather gets really sunny, then the lichen shrivels and stops taking nutriment and moisture from the tree,

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:08.000
but at the same time still prevents it from getting sunburnt.

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:13.000
And when the moisture returns, the lichen can grow back.

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:22.000
So plant and lichen make the best of the two extremes of climate.

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:27.000
Fresh water anywhere on land creates opportunities.

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:36.000
But on volcanic islands like Galapagos, it gets to some very strange places.

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:45.000
Deep in the rocks beneath the Scalasia forest, there is a network of hundreds of tunnels called lava tubes.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:26:04.000
Here, the species-transforming power of the Galapagos is as active as everywhere else.

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:09.000
For scientists like cave-errant Addison and biologist Steve Taylor,

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:14.000
these lava tubes are the Galapagos Islands' new frontier of discovery.

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:25.000
It is difficult to imagine or indeed believe that there are still such untouched areas within a place that's so well known as the Galapagos and so well studied.

00:26:25.000 --> 00:26:43.000
But we do find those areas, and those areas then lead us to new species that are unknown to science or that haven't been described by anyone else ever.

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:50.000
Black volcanic rock still lies only a few inches down beneath the forest trees of Santa Cruz.

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:59.000
It erupted millions of years ago and flowed down the sides of the ancient volcano in rivers of molten red-hot lava.

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:05.000
As the surface of the lava cooled, it solidified and formed a rocky skin.

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:17.000
And when the eruption ceased, the still liquid lava continued to flow away, leaving behind these huge empty caverns.

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:27.000
And now a constant trickle of life-giving water drips down into the winding tunnels.

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:35.000
Steve Taylor is an expert on underground life.

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:43.000
The subterranean world is full of surprises.

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:47.000
It's just really exciting because these animals are pale and eyeless.

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:53.000
There's no selective pressure to maintain eyes in a cave, so they're blind.

00:27:53.000 --> 00:28:02.000
They often have elongated appendages so they can either find prey or avoid prey.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:09.000
This Amplipygid, half scorpion, half spider, is a predator and a scavenger.

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:15.000
It might seem ungainly, but it's well adapted to this black habitat.

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:21.000
Eyes are useless down here, and it's become almost totally blind.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:29.000
Instead, it feels its way through the cave with great skill and sensitivity.

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:41.000
Two of its eight legs are greater elongated and capable of extending to twice the length of its body.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:44.000
This millipede has lost all its color.

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:52.000
They spend precious energy creating a pigment in a place where no one can see it.

00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:58.000
Spiders, too, haunt the lava tubes.

00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:07.000
And just like the tortoises and iguanas, these creatures have evolved into many different species.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:13.000
There are 90 of them, all unique to the Galapagos.

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:19.000
But spiders don't just differ from island to island.

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:26.000
They do so dramatically, even within a single lava tube.

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:31.000
Some that have been here for a long time are blind and feel their way through the cave.

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:39.000
A few have lost their eyes entirely.

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:57.000
But living just centimeters from them are more recent colonists, species that still retain their eyes.

00:29:57.000 --> 00:30:02.000
Such variety in such a small area seems extraordinary.

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:10.000
But in the Galapagos, it's almost common.

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:21.000
The huge number of differing habitats has made Santa Cruz a center of biological diversity.

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:30.000
And as an island ages, so it develops more habitats.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:35.000
Now it's entering its old age.

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:41.000
It's no longer growing.

00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:45.000
Its sheer mass is too heavy for the Earth's crust to support.

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:53.000
It begins to sink under its own weight.

00:30:53.000 --> 00:31:03.000
And now the rainwater that has been falling on it throughout its middle age begins to carve away its substance.

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:13.000
So the island becomes smaller, drier and flatter.

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:22.000
That is what has happened to Española.

00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:30.000
It's nearing four million years old.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:36.000
Its forests have gone.

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:43.000
But it now has a different range of habitats.

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:48.000
Millions of years of erosion have created beaches of soft sand.

00:31:48.000 --> 00:32:03.000
And they suit some animals very well.

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:18.000
This is a natural bathing beach for Galapagos sea lions.

00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:30.000
They are just one of the very few mammal species that are unique to the Galapagos.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:36.000
And the beach of an aging island provides them with an excellent nursery.

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:42.000
Here, sea lion pups can suckle in complete safety.

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:49.000
Though they can be a little irritating.

00:32:49.000 --> 00:33:18.000
And in a protected cove close by the beach, parents can teach their youngsters to swim.

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:30.000
After a swimming lesson, the beach is a perfect place to relax.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:59.000
Sea lions seem to have an idyllic life.

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:03.000
But there is just one irritant.

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:08.000
Flies.

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:15.000
On younger islands with rocky clay slimes, sea lions have help to keep the flies at bay.

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:25.000
Lava lizards.

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:31.000
But on the sandy beaches of Espanola, the lava lizards are nowhere to be seen.

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:39.000
They prefer the nearby rocks, which are warmer.

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:53.000
So here, the sea lions must deal with the fly problem by themselves.

00:34:53.000 --> 00:35:03.000
Espanola's soft sand beaches are also greatly valued by another species.

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:13.000
The waved albatross.

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:21.000
The island provides an excellent nesting ground for these huge sea birds.

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:33.000
With a wingspan of two and a half meters, the albatross is so big and heavy that it has to get up a considerable ground speed in order to take off.

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:40.000
And that's what the beach provides.

00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:49.000
As you might expect, the species of albatross that lives here is slightly different from those found in other parts of the world.

00:35:49.000 --> 00:36:13.000
These wave-like patterns on its neck feathers distinguish it from all other albatross species.

00:36:13.000 --> 00:36:27.000
Albatrosses spend most of their lives on the wing, traveling across entire oceans.

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:39.000
Here on Espanola, the waved albatrosses can nest.

00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:48.000
The isolation of the Galapagos and the protected soft-shingled beaches of Espanola make this aging island an excellent breeding ground for them.

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:54.000
35,000 settle here each year.

00:36:54.000 --> 00:37:00.000
Waved albatrosses are monogamous. They mate for life.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:10.000
But how do you find a new mate or recognize your old partner in such a crowded colony?

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:25.000
You dance.

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:31.000
The whole performance can last for nearly an hour.

00:37:31.000 --> 00:37:37.000
And it's repeated several times every day.

00:37:37.000 --> 00:37:42.000
Sometimes a potential rival steps in to try his luck.

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:59.000
The female in the middle dances with both enthusiastic males at the same time.

00:37:59.000 --> 00:38:06.000
The reward for the victorious male is great.

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:09.000
A mate.

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:19.000
And an opportunity to pass on his genes.

00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:36.000
The many habitats of Espanola and all aging Galapagos islands were created by the erosive power of sea and weather.

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:48.000
But erosion can have only one final result.

00:38:48.000 --> 00:39:03.000
Destruction.

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:18.000
Therefore, a Galapagos island worn down by the waves and the weather eventually reaches the last stage of its existence.

00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:33.000
After millions of years sustaining life, all that remains of it above water is a rocky curving cliff.

00:39:33.000 --> 00:39:46.000
Like Tortuga.

00:39:46.000 --> 00:40:03.000
There are many relic islands like Tortuga in the Galapagos.

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:12.000
Devil's crown in the south of the archipelago is even closer to disappearing altogether below the waves.

00:40:12.000 --> 00:40:20.000
But even in its final days, a Galapagos island provides a habitat for some.

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:23.000
Its rock has been turned by erosion into sediment.

00:40:23.000 --> 00:40:34.000
And now that fertilizes the marine life around his submerged remains.

00:40:34.000 --> 00:40:42.000
A ring of coral two meters wide encircles its dwindling stump.

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:52.000
So a whole new animal community develops.

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:57.000
Corals are at its center.

00:40:57.000 --> 00:41:14.000
Bristle worms hide inside them, occasionally emerging to browse on passing morsels.

00:41:14.000 --> 00:41:18.000
Fish find safety among their branches.

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:38.000
And some of these species once again are unique to the Galapagos.

00:41:38.000 --> 00:42:06.000
Life teams with life.

00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:21.000
But the presence of warm water corals here in the Galapagos seas is something of a surprise.

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:30.000
Because penguins that need cold water live here too.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:36.000
So how can tropical corals and penguins coexist?

00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:43.000
The Galapagos islands have one more trick up their sleeve.

00:42:43.000 --> 00:42:48.000
The archipelago lies at the confluence of several deep ocean currents.

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:53.000
And that creates a bizarre mixture of marine habitats.

00:42:53.000 --> 00:43:03.000
The sub-Antarctic Humboldt current flows around the islands and chills the water just enough for the penguins to survive.

00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:12.000
The corals can't grow in such cold water, but they can go into a state of semi-hibernation for short periods.

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:20.000
When warm water from Central America is dominant, the temperature rises by about seven degrees.

00:43:20.000 --> 00:43:24.000
Now the corals can grow.

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:35.000
And the penguins can find refuge in the few remaining pockets of cold water in the coves and bays that still remain.

00:43:35.000 --> 00:43:43.000
So even in the last stages of its life, the Galapagos island can support a rich animal community.

00:43:43.000 --> 00:43:49.000
But remarkably, even this is not the end of the story.

00:43:49.000 --> 00:44:01.000
Because even when an island has totally disappeared beneath the waves, it continues to influence life in the surrounding seas.

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:10.000
The remains of ancient Galapagos islands stretch for hundreds of miles across the Pacific seabed.

00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:22.000
These were once volcanoes like Fernandina, vegetated mountains like Santa Cruz, and low-lying nurseries like Española.

00:44:22.000 --> 00:44:25.000
Today, those environments are long gone.

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:36.000
But the remnants of the islands under the sea are still key in the lives of one of the ocean's most magnificent inhabitants.

00:44:36.000 --> 00:44:43.000
Up to 12 meters long, it's the largest fish in the world.

00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:49.000
The whale shark.

00:44:49.000 --> 00:44:57.000
Whale sharks come to the Galapagos in large numbers at the same time every year.

00:44:57.000 --> 00:45:08.000
But why they do so is a mystery.

00:45:08.000 --> 00:45:17.000
Marine biologists Alex Hearn and Jonathan Green have spent the last five years trying to solve the puzzle.

00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:23.000
If you think about how Galapagos is formed and how the currents work, the most productive waters are actually out west.

00:45:23.000 --> 00:45:28.000
So you would have thought that if whale sharks were coming here to feed, they'd be out in the west of the archipelago.

00:45:28.000 --> 00:45:31.000
And they're not. They're up north.

00:45:31.000 --> 00:45:37.000
So why are they coming here? It's clearly not to feed.

00:45:37.000 --> 00:45:44.000
What we found out recently is that it's mainly large, pregnant females. Are they coming here to give birth?

00:45:44.000 --> 00:45:48.000
This may be the pupping ground for whale sharks.

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:55.000
I'm slightly skeptical. I think we'd have seen juveniles and we don't.

00:45:55.000 --> 00:46:16.000
So that brings back the question, well, then if they're not pupping here and if they're not feeding here, why are they coming?

00:46:16.000 --> 00:46:33.000
To understand the whale sharks' migratory patterns, Hearn and Green attach satellite tags to the sharks they encounter.

00:46:33.000 --> 00:46:39.000
This enables them to track their movements.

00:46:39.000 --> 00:46:48.000
And it's revealing some extraordinary new facts.

00:46:48.000 --> 00:46:56.000
First, the sharks swim open mouthed through the rich waters off the west coast of South America.

00:46:56.000 --> 00:47:01.000
Then they continue their journey westwards to the Galapagos.

00:47:01.000 --> 00:47:06.000
But they only spend a few days at a time in the island's waters.

00:47:06.000 --> 00:47:19.000
Before continuing westwards out into the open ocean.

00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:23.000
Nobody has yet proved why the whale sharks do this.

00:47:23.000 --> 00:47:28.000
But Alex Hearn has begun to formulate some ideas.

00:47:28.000 --> 00:47:36.000
I think there are two possibilities. Firstly, they may be using Galapagos as a waypoint which directs them towards their pupping grounds.

00:47:36.000 --> 00:47:43.000
The other option is that Galapagos may be providing a service for them along the way and that service may be cleaning.

00:47:43.000 --> 00:47:48.000
Because we do see a lot of cleaning behaviour from the reef fish or maybe a combination of the two.

00:47:48.000 --> 00:47:58.000
The long line of submerged Galapagos islands could play a central role in the whale sharks' extraordinary journey.

00:47:58.000 --> 00:48:03.000
It might be that they serve as signposts by which the sharks navigate.

00:48:03.000 --> 00:48:11.000
If you start looking at where they're going, especially the tracks that we have along those ridges and then up to the next ridge and then back down again,

00:48:11.000 --> 00:48:22.000
it certainly seems that they're associating with those ridges for one reason or another. That could be geomagnetism or it could also be something to do with the biology of the water column above those ridges.

00:48:22.000 --> 00:48:41.000
But certainly something is going on.

00:48:41.000 --> 00:48:49.000
From their birth to their death, the islands have acted like evolutionary pressure cookers.

00:48:49.000 --> 00:49:00.000
From the youngest islands like Fernandina, middle-aged ones like Santa Cruz and the old islands like Española.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:11.000
They're extremely varied with contrasted conditions.

00:49:11.000 --> 00:49:40.000
Deserts, rainforests and polar waters crowded together in a very small area.

00:49:40.000 --> 00:50:06.000
These huge variations have created a wide range of opportunities for the few animals that have managed to reach here.

00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:26.000
As they colonized, so they adapted and consequently flourished.

00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:40.000
That explains many of the oddities of the inhabitants of these islands.

00:50:40.000 --> 00:50:53.000
Including that most fundamental phenomenon of all, the appearance of new species.

00:50:53.000 --> 00:51:01.000
The giant tortoise is a very emblem of the Galapagos and in their heyday there were hundreds of thousands of them.

00:51:01.000 --> 00:51:07.000
Not only that, there were 15 different species each in its own locality.

00:51:07.000 --> 00:51:13.000
But why should there be so many species within such a comparatively restricted area?

00:51:13.000 --> 00:51:25.000
In the next program we'll look at the deep geological forces that can make a single species produce many and turn the Galapagos into this wonderland.
